<div style="background-color: #1a5276; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; color: white; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How to Market a Business Idea</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program. Interactive: Respond to questions and arguments in debates.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret instructions on using a computer program to present an original innovation (e.g., explains the steps for creating a presentation, such as "selecting a template layout, organizing key features on each slide, and adding images of the invention").
            Can respond to classmates' questions during a debate on whether Panamanian businesses should rely on automated customer service (e.g., "I think chatbots make communication more efficient, but they lack the human touch that customers expect.")
            Can ask for clarification when debating complex topics, like technology's impact on privacy (e.g., "What do you mean by 'data security'?").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, and introducing core vocabulary and pronunciation in an accessible way, preparing them for the lesson's listening focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of successful Panamanian businesses (e.g., a local coffee shop, a small tech startup, a craft market stall) and asks students: "What makes these businesses successful? How do they tell people about their products?" Students discuss in pairs for 2 minutes. The teacher then writes the key vocabulary on the board: <i>innovation, strategy, marketing, customer base, efficiency, launch, promote, engage, success, entrepreneurial</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of the new vocabulary, paying special attention to the /ɔː/ sound in words like 'launch' and 'cost' (if introduced with related examples) and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words such as 'entrepreneurial' /ˌɒn.trə.prəˈnɜːr.i.əl/ and 'efficiency' /ɪˈfɪʃ.ən.si/. The teacher says each word clearly and asks students to repeat chorally and individually. For example, "Listen: 'launch' /lɔːntʃ/. Repeat: 'launch'." "Listen: 'entrepreneurial'. Repeat: 'entrepreneurial'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the core vocabulary.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a new app is created, what do we call that process?" (Expected: innovation)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a company launches a new product, what are they doing with it for the first time?" (Expected: making it available, introducing it to the market)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage introduces new language content through an authentic listening text related to the "Entrepreneurial Spirit" scenario, specifically focusing on marketing business ideas. Students engage in initial comprehension tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher introduces an audio clip (approximately 2-3 minutes long) featuring an interview with a young Panamanian entrepreneur discussing how they market their tech innovation. The audio includes instructions on using a presentation software to showcase their product and mentions strategies for customer engagement. The audio naturally integrates the target vocabulary and grammatical structures (First conditional, Present perfect for innovations, Future continuous).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of an interview (e.g., a podcast segment or a radio snippet) with a clear, professional register, focusing on business strategies and technological instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Listening - Gist):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the audio once and answer the gist question: "What is the main topic of the entrepreneur's discussion?" (A: Marketing a new business idea / A new tech innovation).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen again and identify three different marketing strategies mentioned. (e.g., social media advertising, promotional events, collaboration with influencers).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in listening comprehension, specifically targeting the new vocabulary, grammar structures, and pronunciation points introduced in the presentation stage. Activities progress from controlled to semi-controlled practice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details (vocabulary: *innovation, strategy, marketing, customer base, efficiency, launch, promote, engage, success, entrepreneurial*; grammar: First conditional, Present perfect for innovations, Future continuous).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill while listening):</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences from the audio, each containing a gap where one of the target vocabulary words or a specific grammatical structure should be. Students listen to specific segments of the audio again and fill in the blanks.
                            <br>Example: "If we __________ on social media, we might reach more customers." (advertise)
                            <br>Example: "Local shops have begun using social media to tell more people about their __________." (products)
                            <br>Example: "I'll be analyzing the market __________ next week." (trends)
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Instruction Interpretation):</b> The teacher plays short snippets of the audio where the entrepreneur gives instructions on using presentation software. Students listen and order a list of jumbled steps for creating a presentation (e.g., "selecting a template layout," "organizing key features on each slide," "adding images of the invention"). This directly addresses the specific objective: "Interpret instructions for more complex technology."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Vocabulary and Pronunciation):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a list of the core vocabulary words. They take turns saying the words, focusing on correct pronunciation (especially /ɔː/ and multisyllabic words), while their partner listens and checks for accuracy. They then discuss what each word means in the context of the audio clip. For example, Student A says "launch," Student B listens and confirms pronunciation, then Student A explains: "To launch a business means to start it or introduce a new product."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In this fluency-focused stage, students actively use the target language and listening skills in a realistic, collaborative communicative task related to the "Entrepreneurial Spirit" scenario and "How to Market a Business Idea" theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Short, informal debate/discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). The teacher presents a statement related to marketing and technology, e.g., "Panamanian businesses should rely heavily on automated customer service to increase efficiency and reduce costs."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups spend 2 minutes discussing their initial thoughts.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then facilitates a mini-debate. The teacher reads arguments for and against the statement (e.g., "Automated service increases efficiency, but it lacks the human touch" or "If we automate, we can serve more customers globally").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to each argument and take turns responding, expressing agreement or disagreement, and asking for clarification using phrases like: "I think chatbots make communication more efficient, but they lack the human touch that customers expect." or "What do you mean by 'data security' when we talk about automated systems?" They use target vocabulary such as *efficiency, costs, customer base, technology, automation*.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on active listening, responding appropriately, and communicating ideas clearly, not necessarily "winning" the debate.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on formative assessment to gauge student learning and provides an opportunity for students to self-reflect on their listening skills and use of new language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to interpret instructions, respond to arguments, and ask for clarification during the debate and other listening tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher presents a final short audio clip (1 minute) describing a new, innovative product idea (e.g., a solar-powered charging station for rural areas). After listening, students must individually write down two marketing strategies they would recommend and one question they have about its target customer base or potential challenges, demonstrating their ability to interpret and process information from listening input. They should use at least three of the target vocabulary words in their response.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify key information from the listening text (e.g., product features, marketing challenges).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., *innovation, strategy, customer base*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of recommended strategies and questions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement and collaboration during group discussions/debates.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new marketing strategy or business term I learned to understand today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging for me when listening to the instructions or the debate?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and provides a bridge to the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel interpreting instructions for a new app or computer program now, after listening to the entrepreneur?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific aspect of marketing a business idea do I want to learn more about in our next lesson?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., the audio clip's authenticity, student engagement in the debate).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., provide a more detailed vocabulary glossary for struggling students, offer more structured sentence frames for debate responses).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short article or blog post that further explores some of the marketing strategies and challenges discussed today, using the same core vocabulary introduced in this listening lesson. This will provide a different modality to reinforce understanding of the topic and the new words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>